INT. COUNTRY HOUSE LIBRARY - NIGHT

A room of leather and dust. A fire gone to embers. An oak desk, a reel-to-reel recorder, a glass of sherry with a lipstick ring. The French windows are shut, the curtains drawn.

AGATHA PENHALLOW, 60s, silver hair, pearls, black gloves, sits upright at the desk with her hands folded. She presses a button. The reels turn.

AGATHA (V.O.)
(recorded, younger, brisk)
Tape one. Tuesday the fourteenth. I begin this record of my own free will and in sound mind.

She stops the tape. Rewinds a few inches. Presses play.

AGATHA (V.O.)
(recorded)
Tuesday the fourteenth. I begin this record of my own free will and in sound mind.

She smiles faintly and stops it again.

AGATHA
Nobody ever believes a woman in pearls.

She pours a little sherry. Does not drink it.

AGATHA (CONT'D)
Quite right. Let me begin properly.

She flips a switch. A red light glows.

AGATHA (CONT'D)
My name is Agatha Penhallow. On the night of the fourteenth, my husband Lionel was found dead in this room. The coroner said his heart. The vicar said providence. The maid said nothing at all, which is why I trusted her.

She leans back.

AGATHA (CONT'D)
I should say first that I did not like Lionel. I should say second that I did not kill him. And I should say third, since these things are the sort that must be said third, that I know who did.

A beat. The fire ticks.

AGATHA (CONT'D)
It was the sherry. Lionel's sherry, from the decanter on that shelf. He never drank mine. He thought I would poison him. Quite the joke, in the end.

She nods at the glass.

AGATHA (CONT'D)
Someone who knew his habits, his decanter, and his hour. Someone who stood in this room while he drank and did not call for help. Someone with a key to the cellar and a reason to forget it.

She lifts the glass, turns it in the firelight.

AGATHA (CONT'D)
I tell you this, whoever you are, listening to this tape, because I have no one else to tell. The police have lost interest. The family has lost heart. And I have, I regret to say, lost my husband's will.

She sets the glass down. Presses stop.

For a time, nothing. The embers settle. A clock in the hall strikes twelve.

She presses play again.

AGATHA (V.O.)
(recorded, in a different, older voice)
Agatha, if you are hearing this, it is because I have already done it. Lionel did not drink the sherry. You did.

Agatha stares at the machine. Her gloved hand finds the edge of the desk.

AGATHA (V.O.)
(recorded)
I have been recording you for twenty years, my dear. Every night after supper. You never noticed the machine under the floorboard.

AGATHA
(whispering)
Lionel.

AGATHA (V.O.)
(recorded)
You will want to know where it is. Look under the third book on the second shelf. It is not a book.

She rises. Crosses to the shelf. Counts. Pulls the third book. It is a hollow box. Inside, a second reel, labeled in Lionel's tight script: AGATHA, THE TRUTH.

She holds it. Her hands shake.

AGATHA
(to herself)
Oh, Lionel. You clever, clever man.

She carries the reel back to the desk and threads it onto the spindle. The machine whirs.

AGATHA (V.O.)
(recorded, her own voice again, younger, laughing)
Lionel, you have a terrible habit of listening at keyholes.

Agatha's face goes white. She reaches for the switch.

AGATHA (V.O.)
(recorded)
Tape one, take two. Let us begin again.

She presses stop. Silence. She looks at the glass of sherry, the lipstick ring, the dying fire. Then, very slowly, she lifts the glass to her lips and drinks.

AGATHA
Tuesday the fourteenth.
